{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793305899,"nonce":"N7lf/4eT2GfsDb8I9As7OZIbroQ=","message":"Prioritized as item 2 of the WASM compilation-speed effort (2026-10-29). Reuse this issue rather than creating a duplicate. Each WindowPage worker eagerly parses the full 23 MiB stream and then strips bodies; recorded preparation is 1.6-2.8 s per worker, running concurrently. Thousands of small bodies are also offered to every shard before sweeping.\n\nInvestigate lazy body materialization or preselected shard IR/import sets, retaining only owned functions and transitively required inlining candidates. Body size/call summaries can be computed once in the original module. Ensure globals, block addresses, personalities, debug metadata, exception shields and available_externally semantics remain correct. Avoid merely moving all eager parsing into another serial stage. The current LLVM C API lacks individual-function materialization; any bridge must use the supported LLVM version and ownership rules.\n\nAcceptance: bounded peak RSS, per-worker preparation/optimization/codegen A/B timings, identical runtime behavior (native, WASM fibers and no fibers, exceptions and reflection), and complete browser smoke. Related cache issue: 8b12bc8. Coordinate deterministic partition and dependency identities with the balancing work. No measured improvement yet.","files":null}]}